  "title": "Food",
  "description": "Learn essential French words for food and drink, and how to say what you want, eat, and drink.",
  "guidebook": "Welcome to **Food**! 🥐\n\nIn French, every noun has a **gender**. Masculine nouns use *le* (or *un*), feminine nouns use *la* (or *une*). For example, *le café* (the coffee) is masculine, but *la pizza* is feminine.\n\nTo say what you want, use **je veux** (I want): *Je veux du café* — I want (some) coffee. **Du** is the partitive article, used for unspecified quantities of food and drink.\n\nTo say what you eat or drink, use **je mange** (I eat) and **je bois** (I drink): *Je mange du riz* (I eat rice), *Je bois du jus* (I drink juice).\n\n**Boire** is irregular: *je bois, tu bois, il boit*. **Manger** is regular but adds *-e* before silent endings: *je mange, tu manges*. Bon appétit! 🍽️",
